From 6aba412ee132c49a0ca4dd97949bd23c6a6df6c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 20:59:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Crash reports: catch it, write it, and offer to send it

exception_unhandled_handler is installed as the first line of obj_game's
Create - before the assets, the audio and the network layer, because a
missing sprite or a driver that will not hand over a surface is exactly
the sort of thing that goes wrong on somebody else's machine, and a
handler installed halfway down the file catches the wrong half.

The report is the timestamp, the version, the OS and display, the
exception with its script, line and stack trace, the save slots by name
and size, and the last forty lines of the net log when there is one. The
saves are there because a crash that only happens with one particular
save is otherwise pure guesswork - the name is what lets you ask for the
right file. It is also the one part of the report the player wrote
themselves, which is a reason of its own to ask before sending.

Written to settlers_crash.txt beside the saves and nothing more: no
network call from inside a dying runtime, which is how a crash report
becomes a hang. The next launch is what deals with it.

NOTHING IN HERE CARRIES A CREDENTIAL, and nothing should. Everything in
the executable belongs to whoever downloaded it, so an SMTP password or a
Discord account token in the game is that account in a stranger's hands
within a week of anyone caring to look - and for Discord it is the whole
account, every server and every DM, on top of being against their rules.
What goes in the build is a write-only endpoint instead:

  CRASH_REPORT_STYLE "relay"    JSON { subject, message, access_key } to a
                                form relay, which emails it on. The
                                destination address lives at the service,
                                so it changes without shipping a build.
  CRASH_REPORT_STYLE "discord"  JSON { content } to a channel webhook: a
                                URL that can post into one channel and do
                                nothing else. Deleted and reissued if it
                                is ever abused.

Discord rejects anything over 2000 characters, so a report goes in a code
fence and is trimmed to 1800 - not something nearer the limit, because the
header and the fence sit outside that count and the version string is not
a fixed width. The report is written most-useful-first, so what gets
trimmed is the tail of the net log.

CRASH_REPORT_URL is EMPTY, deliberately: a URL I invented would fail
silently forever. Empty is a working state - the report is written, the
game says where the file is, and nobody is asked anything. Filling in the
macro is the whole of turning it on.

It asks before sending, once, on the launch after a crash, and remembers
the answer - including a no, because asking every launch is just wearing
somebody down. The file is deleted once sent or refused so it cannot go
twice; a failed send keeps it for next time.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
